.TH WREX-FRAG 7 "2026-10-18" "wrex 0.1.0" "wrex Manual"
.SH NAME
wrex-frag \- frag layer for \fBwrex\fR(1)

.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBwrex\fR \fIcraft\fR|\fIsend\fR [options] \fB--layer\fR \fIfrag\fR \fB--frag\-<field>\fR=\fIVALUE\fR ...
\fBwrex\fR \fIfields\fR \fIfrag\fR

.SH DESCRIPTION
IPv6 fragment header (RFC 8200 §4.5, next\-header 44): fixed 8 bytes — next_header, reserved, the packed u16 (13\-bit offset | 2 reserved bits | M flag) and the 32\-bit ident. offset and m are bit windows over the packed u16 (the ipv4 flags/frag_off pattern; over\-wide offset values mask to 13 bits). The header chains into next_header ONLY at offset 0 — a non\-initial fragment carries no upper\-layer header, so offset > 0 is terminal with the fragment bytes as payload. Dissect\-only view: reassembly is a deferral (analyzer work).
.

.SH ENCAPSULATION
Rank 25; stacks on ipv6, hopopts, routing, frag, dstopts, ah (parents).

.SH "FIELD FLAGS"
Dots in field paths become dashes in flags (ipv4.ttl -> --ipv4-ttl, --frag-next_header).
Explicit values always win over autofill and are kept
unvalidated; \fB\-\-strict\fR re-validates (violations exit 2).
.TP
\fB\-\-frag\-next_header=\fIINT\fR\fR
next header protocol of the fragmentable part (autofill: derived from the next layer) Type u8. Default: 59.
.TP
\fB\-\-frag\-offset=\fIINT\fR\fR
fragment offset in 8\-octet units (13 bits; over\-wide values mask) Type u16. Default: 0.
.TP
\fB\-\-frag\-m=\fIINT\fR\fR
more\-fragments flag (1 bit: 0 = last fragment) Type u8. Default: 0.
.TP
\fB\-\-frag\-ident=\fIINT\fR\fR
fragment identification (matches fragments of one packet) Type u32. Default: 0.

.SH EXAMPLES
wrex fields frag

